Collins holds a large fundraising and incumbency edge"], "rationale": "Recent polls from mid-September through early October consistently put Jackson slightly ahead, averaging roughly +1.5 to +2, and markets and several raters lean Democratic, though many still call it a Tossup. Collins's incumbency, name recognition and $11 million war chest keep this close, so a modest Democratic edge with wide uncertainty is warranted.", "watch": "Post-debate polling and any late outside spending surge for Collins could shift a race this tight.", "confidence": "medium", "notes": ["Incumbent Susan Collins (R) is running."]}, "overview": "Maine's Senate race pits four-term incumbent Republican Susan Collins against Democratic state legislator Troy Jackson in a contest most analysts rate a Tossup, though several recent ratings have moved to Lean Democratic. Polling since mid-September shows Jackson narrowly ahead in most surveys, with averages clustering around a one-to-two point Democratic margin. The first debate on October 7 featured sharp clashes over Trump, abortion and trade, with Collins working to distance herself from the president.\n\nCollins retains significant advantages: a $11 million cash-on-hand lead, decades of incumbency, and a history of outperforming her party in Maine. But the state's Democratic lean and a favorable national environment for Democrats have put her on the defensive. Markets give Jackson roughly a 56-57% chance, and the race remains well within reach for either candidate heading into the final weeks.", "history": [{"date": "2026-09-22", "p_dem": 0.7341, "margin": 3.16}, {"date": "2026-09-23", "p_dem": 0.6906, "margin": 2.5}, {"date": "2026-09-24", "p_dem": 0.626, "margin": 1.5}, {"date": "2026-09-25", "p_dem": 0.6915, "margin": 2.5}, {"date": "2026-09-26", "p_dem": 0.5751, "margin": 1.5}, {"date": "2026-09-27", "p_dem": 0.6364, "margin": 2.5}, {"date": "2026-09-28", "p_dem": 0.6474, "margin": 2.0}, {"date": "2026-09-29", "p_dem": 0.6668, "margin": 2